# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
aquinas-third-way.md:1: argue/readings: S5 is read 2 ways: aquinas-third-way.logic.r3 and aquinas-third-way.logic.r3.3 have "begin_needs_existing"; aquinas-third-way.logic.r3.2 has "begin_needs_existing" and "∃t ∀x ¬exists_at(x, t) ∧ begin_needs_existing → ¬something_began"; they differ when only "that which does not exist only begins to exist by something already existing" and "something has begun to exist" are true, which the first allows
aquinas-third-way.md:1: argue/answered: S5: answered yes, which keeps aquinas-third-way.logic.r3 and aquinas-third-way.logic.r3.3, to: Suppose "that which does not exist only begins to exist by something already existing" and "something has begun to exist" are true. Is S5, "Now if this were true, even now there would be nothing in existence, because that which does not exist only begins to exist by something already existing.", true then?
aquinas-third-way.md:1: argue/readings: S6 is read 3 ways: aquinas-third-way.logic.r3 has "begin_needs_existing → (∃t ∀x ¬exists_at(x, t) → ¬something_began)" and "∃t ∀x ¬exists_at(x, t) ∧ ¬something_began → now_nothing" and "¬now_nothing"; aquinas-third-way.logic.r3.2 has "¬something_began → now_nothing" and "¬now_nothing"; they differ when only "that which does not exist only begins to exist by something already existing" is true, which the first allows; aquinas-third-way.logic.r3.3 has "begin_needs_existing → (∃t ∀x ¬exists_at(x, t) → ¬something_began)" and "¬something_began → now_nothing" and "¬now_nothing"
aquinas-third-way.md:1: argue/answered: S6: answered yes, which keeps aquinas-third-way.logic.r3, to: Suppose "that which does not exist only begins to exist by something already existing" is true and "something has begun to exist" and "even now nothing would be in existence" are false. Is S6, "Therefore, if at one time nothing was in existence, it would have been impossible for anything to have begun to exist; and thus even now nothing would be in existence--which is absurd.", true then?
aquinas-third-way.logic.r3: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 2 ways: aquinas-third-way.logic.r3 and aquinas-third-way.logic.r3.3 have "∀x (¬contingent(x) → necessary(x))" and "∀x (necessary(x) → caused_nec(x)) ∧ ∃x necessary(x) → infinite_regress"; aquinas-third-way.logic.r3.2 has "∀x (¬contingent(x) → necessary(x))" and "∀x (necessary(x) → caused_nec(x)) → infinite_regress"; they differ when nothing is true, which the first allows
aquinas-third-way.logic.r3: argue/readings: U1: aquinas-third-way.logic.r3.2 takes for granted "if (for every x, if the existence of x is necessary, then x has its necessity caused by another), then there is a going on to infinity in necessary things which have their necessity caused by another"; aquinas-third-way.logic.r3 and aquinas-third-way.logic.r3.3 do not
aquinas-third-way.logic.r3: argue/answered: U1: answered yes, which keeps aquinas-third-way.logic.r3.2, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "if (for every x, if the existence of x is necessary, then x has its necessity caused by another), then there is a going on to infinity in necessary things which have their necessity caused by another"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
aquinas-third-way.md:1: argue/negation: aquinas-third-way.logic.r3 P3 (S3): the sentence says "impossible", but P3 denies nothing it speaks of: it reads "for every x, if x is possible to be and not to be, then (for some t, it is not so that x is in existence at time t)"
aquinas-third-way.md:1: argue/unfaithful: aquinas-third-way.logic.r3 P10 (S11): the sentence has no word of "x has its necessity caused by another": is it the sentence P10 came from?
aquinas-third-way.md:1: argue/unfaithful: aquinas-third-way.logic.r3 P10 (S11): the sentence has no word of "the existence of x is necessary": is it the sentence P10 came from?
aquinas-third-way.md:1: argue/quantifier: aquinas-third-way.logic.r3 C (S11): the sentence says "all", but C says only some: it reads "for some x, x is what all men speak of as God"
